{
  "name": "gateway",
  "main": "./src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-04-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  // `JWT_SECRET` and `DATABASE_URL` arrive through the deploy job's `--var`
  // flags, from GitHub secrets, and the reason for that is one word: a secret
  // must never be in source control. It is NOT because `--var` displaces this
  // map. `wrangler deploy --var` MERGES into `vars` rather than replacing it —
  // `wrangler deploy --var DATABASE_URL:… --dry-run` in `workers/wallet`
  // publishes that worker's seven declared vars alongside `DATABASE_URL`, on
  // 4.107.0 and again on 4.147.0.
  //
  // So the split is by sensitivity and nothing else: anything secret is a
  // workflow flag, anything not is declared right here, which is the same
  // arrangement `workers/wallet/wrangler.jsonc` uses and for the same reason.
  // `wrangler-config.contract.test.ts` is what keeps the two halves in agreement
  // — it fails if a var is read here and declared in neither place.
  //
  // `DATABASE_URL` arrived with `/p/{slug}`, the public per-merchant page a
  // Google class review opens. It is read in the gateway rather than reached
  // through a service binding on purpose: every `/v1/*` route requires a JWT and
  // this one is public by requirement, so routing it through the binding chain
  // would mean inventing an auth bypass. It is the same Neon connection string,
  // as a plain var, that `identity`, `wallet` and `growth` already receive.
  // See `apps/gateway/src/merchant-store.ts`.
  "vars": {
    // The `apps/customer-web` origin, for the one CTA on `/p/{slug}`.
    //
    // `/p/*` and `/brand/*` are gateway routes; `/register` and `/profile` are
    // `apps/customer-web` pages this worker does not serve. So this host has to
    // be named separately from the gateway's own origin — building the CTA on
    // `media.juancode.dev` renders a page that looks right and a link that 404s,
    // which is the `programLogo` defect at a different URL.
    //
    // The pages.dev host, not a custom domain, because those two routes exist in
    // `apps/customer-web/app` and are served from there. Optional with a
    // documented default in `parseCustomerWebBaseUrl`, but DECLARED: a value
    // that only exists as a fallback in code is invisible to
    // `wrangler-config.contract.test.ts`, and a deployment whose CTA host was
    // never configured is indistinguishable from one where it was configured
    // correctly. It holds the same value as `DEFAULT_WALLET_CUSTOMER_WEB_BASE_URL`
    // in `src/merchant-page.ts`, and `wrangler-config.contract.test.ts` asserts
    // the two agree — so a staging deployment that moves this host and leaves the
    // constant behind fails here instead of drifting.
    "WALLET_CUSTOMER_WEB_BASE_URL": "https://vyloop-customer-web.pages.dev"
  },
  // Static assets, and this block is load-bearing rather than decorative.
  //
  // `./public/` is NOT served by default. Without an `assets` block wrangler
  // never uploads the directory, so `public/robots.txt` would exist in the repo
  // and answer 404 in production — a crawler file that lies about being there.
  //
  // `run_worker_first: ["/robots.txt"]` sends exactly one path to the Worker
  // script first, and the Worker then serves the file through the `ASSETS`
  // binding (`src/robots.ts`). The alternative — asset-first, Worker never
  // invoked — would be marginally cheaper and completely untestable: the
  // robots.txt contract is that Google can GET it and get
  // `text/plain`, and asserting that through `app.fetch` requires a real route
  // to fetch. One extra Worker invocation for a document crawlers fetch about
  // once a day per host is the right trade for a rule that has to be verifiable.
  //
  // `not_found_handling: "none"` is spelled out because it is what keeps this
  // block from changing anything else. On an unmatched request the Worker runs,
  // exactly as it did before assets existed, so no existing route is shadowed by
  // the assets layer. Anyone tempted to switch this to "single-page-application"
  // would silently swallow every unmatched gateway route into a 200.
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./public/",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "none",
    "run_worker_first": ["/robots.txt"]
  },
  // Brand assets. The bucket is private: this route is the only public door, so the
  // r2.dev preview URL is never what Google fetches and disabling it breaks nothing.
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "BRAND_ASSETS",
      "bucket_name": "vyloop-brand-assets"
    }
  ],
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "RATE_LIMITER",
      "id": "c2d6e3da0e96495697b3caa2c4270e87"
    }
  ],
  "queues": {
    "producers": [
      {
        "binding": "EVENT_QUEUE",
        "queue": "vyloop-events-dev"
      },
      {
        "binding": "DOMAIN_EVENTS_QUEUE",
        "queue": "vyloop-domain-events"
      }
    ]
  },
  "services": [
    { "binding": "identity", "service": "identity" },
    { "binding": "customer", "service": "customer" },
    { "binding": "growth", "service": "growth" },
    { "binding": "analytics", "service": "analytics" },
    { "binding": "automation", "service": "automation" },
    { "binding": "communication", "service": "communication" },
    { "binding": "compliance", "service": "compliance" },
    { "binding": "wallet", "service": "wallet" }
  ]
}
